From dab423f60e390ac1a1b54d89baefb89a412ed2f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:51:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] Settings of a film kept by what it is:
 identity, tracks and releases

Kodi keeps the settings of a video (its audio and subtitle tracks, their
delays, the zoom, the picture settings) for its file, by its path. An
add-on that plays from an online service gives another link at each
playback, often of another release of the same film, so those settings
were lost every time.

FilmMemory.h is the logic, with no state, to keep them for the film or
episode itself, named by the ids its add-on gives it (IMDb, TMDb, TVDb;
season and number for an episode), one record per release that played
(its length and the format of its video):
- two identities are the same video only with an id in common and no id
  of the same type that differs, the same kind, season and number;
- tracks are found again by what they are (language and its variety,
  forced, commentary, audio description, then name, codec, channels,
  hearing impaired, place among those of their language), never by their
  number, and only where the release that plays has such a track;
- each setting comes from the most recent record where it applies: the
  tracks from any release, the zoom from a picture of the same shape, the
  picture settings from a video of the same format, the volume from the
  same cut (lengths within 0.5 %, 5 to 30 s), the audio and subtitle
  delays and the video stream only from the same release (the same length
  to the second and the same video), as another release or another cut is
  timed otherwise;
- a record is a versioned "key=value" text: a later version's keys are
  left out and values not valid here keep the default.

TestFilmMemory covers the identities, the text, the tracks and what each
release takes.
